{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcSpEzqopXETu7N3d4cV", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Yes, but only with `--dry-run`.\n\nA team memory flags a hazard here: in staging, `EXPORT_BUCKET` points at the **production** bucket. If you run the export against staging without `--dry-run`, it writes to prod.\n\n`scripts/export.py` reads the bucket from the environment (`scripts/export.py:10`), so the repo can't tell me what staging sets it to. I couldn't confirm the hazard myself, which means it's still in effect. The script does accept `--dry-run` (`scripts/export.py:8`) and prints the target bucket before it does anything:\n\n```\npython scripts/export.py --env staging --dry-run\n```\n\n(I'm assuming the flag is `--env` because the script uses `args.env`. Check `--help` if that's wrong.)\n\nLook at the `exporting staging to <bucket>` line in the output. If it names the prod bucket, the memory is still accurate. To do a real (non-dry) staging test, override the bucket to a non-prod one for that run, e.g. `EXPORT_BUCKET=ledgerline-exports-dev python scripts/export.py --env staging`, and check the printed bucket first."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 714, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24860, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 714}, "output_tokens": 381, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a1300928-1737-4f4d-858f-9e16922cb877", "uuid": "cecaf17a-ad8c-4344-a02a-cf5afad4d7b7", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:14:48.664Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcSpEgjwzaVLVxucALWi"}
